st  rector's well every direct is different  that's the truth there's no there is no  normal or usual way about it everybody  is very unique but he thinks like an  editor so and he works very quickly  frustratingly quickly sometimes in terms  of he'll will do one take and he'll go  okay we've got it when you move on I now  say well how can we do another take and  he goes no I only need it for this bit  and I got you we got that bit so he's  thinks in a different way you know I  think for the actors you you want to  perfect every take from beginning to end  and you want to have a few goes at it  and but he but it was more it was more  you could tell you were being directed  by somebody who came at it from from  editing for sure very very important in  some of these sequences that I'm not  gonna spoil yet yeah I also wanted to  ask you about balancing Kubrick's  interpretation of the story with Stephen  King's I know that was on Mike shoulders  oh yes but did you have that in mind at  all when you were approaching bringing I  worried about it before we started  shooting because I thought that's that's  where we could that's that that is the  hurdle that Mike had to come get over  was the fact that there are two there  are the people who love the novel and  there are people who love Kubrick's  movie and they're not necessarily in  each other they don't cross over a lot  because Kubrick made a lot of changes to  the story that Stephen King didn't like  and and I think a lot of fans of the  novel felt like didn't you know weren't  happy with that so I worried that well  we're it's gonna be seen to be in the  follow-on movie from The Shining how are  we going to please everybody go but he  managed to do it beautifully Mike with  our fourth act he did something very  very clever  and at the end of our movie which sort  of gives the fans of Stephen King's  novel who were aggrieved by Kubrick's  movie he sort of gives them he gives  them what they were missing I couldn't  ag